//! Complete frame descriptions returned by a game's `draw` function.

/// An opaque frame description.
type t = host

/// Create an unlit frame from a camera and scene.
let create : (Camera3D.t, Scene.t) => t
/// Create a lit frame from a camera, scene, and lights.
let createLit : (Camera3D.t, Scene.t, List<Light.t>) => t
/// Create a standalone 2D frame from a camera and sprite tree.
let create2D : (Camera2D.t, Sprite.t) => t
/// Render another frame into a target before rendering the main frame.
///
/// The target frame is a complete `Frame.create` / `Frame.createLit` with its
/// OWN lights, so a lit or shadowed feed needs `createLit` plus
/// `Light.castShadows` there. A scene that samples the very target it is drawn
/// into sees the previous frame's image. The main frame is last for piping.
let withRenderTarget : (RenderTarget.t, t, t) => t
/// Paint a UI view into a target before rendering the main frame.
///
/// The view is a `Ui.*` tree painted at the target's declared size and read
/// back like any render target (`Scene.quad() |> Scene.screen(target)`) — a
/// monitor mesh, a cockpit panel. Views on targets are display-only for now:
/// interactive widgets (buttons, sliders, text inputs) render, but their
/// handlers are ignored. The screen clears to the engine's default
/// background. Like `withRenderTarget`, the FIRST declaration of a target id
/// wins; one id must not be declared by both writers. Runs on every shell —
/// native, web, and VR. The main frame is last for piping.
let withUiTarget : (RenderTarget.t, Ui.view, t) => t
/// Attach fog to a frame; the frame is last for piping.
let withFog : (Fog.t, t) => t
/// Attach a cubemap skybox to a frame; the frame is last for piping.
let withSkybox : (Skybox.t, t) => t
/// Override a frame's background clear color.
///
/// This overrides the default of clearing to the fog color, and paints the
/// background only — it does not change how fog blends over geometry.
let withClearColor : (Color.t, t) => t
/// Show the real world behind a frame — mixed reality.
///
/// The background becomes the headset's camera feed: the skybox and clear
/// color are dropped, and geometry composites over the room by its alpha:
/// opaque surfaces hide it, textured or not (a texture's own alpha is
/// ignored), while `Scene.opacity` subtrees and translucent 2D sprites let it
/// through. The
/// feed itself is requested by `config`'s `Xr.passthrough(true)`. Wherever
/// no feed is composited — desktop, web, or a headset with the feed paused
/// or unavailable — the background is a stand-in room: a darker floor at
/// `y = 0` with a faint 1 m grid, grey walls above the horizon. The frame is
/// last for piping.
let withPassthrough : (t) => t
/// Stand-in geometry for the real room behind a passthrough frame; the frame
/// is last for piping.
///
/// The room is ordinary scene geometry in world units (metres, floor at
/// `y = 0`), drawn with the frame's scene only where the stand-in room is —
/// desktop, web, or a headset whose camera feed is not composited — so a
/// desk or a wall the game expects reads without a headset. It occludes the
/// scene like real geometry would, which a camera feed never does. Under
/// `Xr.scene(true)` its depth is also the environment depth there, standing
/// in for the headset's depth sensing; the procedural floor behind it is not,
/// so declare a floor here if one should count. A frame without
/// `withPassthrough` ignores it.
let withStandInRoom : (Scene.t, t) => t
/// Shade a world-aligned grid onto the room's surfaces; the frame is last for
/// piping.
///
/// A fullscreen pass reads the environment depth that `config`'s
/// `Xr.scene(true)` provides — the headset's depth sensing under a
/// composited camera feed, the frame's `withStandInRoom` geometry elsewhere —
/// recovers the world point behind each pixel, and draws thin lines every
/// `cell` world units (metres in passthrough) on whichever axis planes the
/// surface faces, fading out 4–8 m away. The lines blend over the room and
/// under the frame's scene. `cell` must be positive. Without `Xr.scene(true)`
/// nothing is drawn.
let withSurfaceGrid : (float, Color.t, t) => t
/// Add an ordered sprite pass above the frame's 3D scene.
///
/// Layers render in call order, so a second `with2D` draws above the first.
/// The main frame is last for piping.
let with2D : (Camera2D.t, Sprite.t, t) => t

/// Compare two frames structurally — the escape hatch for `Frame.t`, which is
/// opaque and therefore supports no `==`.
///
/// Compares every part of the frame: camera, scene, lights, render-target
/// passes, fog, skybox, clear color, passthrough, and 2D layers (all ordered). It also
/// distinguishes HOW the frame was built — a `Frame.create2D` frame is never
/// equal to a 3D frame carrying the same layer through `with2D`. Intended for
/// inline `expect` tests over `draw` output, NOT for per-frame logic — the
/// walk is O(frame size).
///
/// It inherits `Scene.equals`'s rules: floats compare exactly, assets compare
/// by locator rather than by loaded content, animation compares as declared,
/// and a failing `expect` can only report THAT the two frames differ — assert
/// over the smallest piece that makes the point.
let equals : (t, t) => bool
